  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 438 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe weather remains fairly benign today and tomorrow, as a strong\nupper level ridge continues to dominate over Montana. This ridge \nwill allow temperatures to remain abnormally warm and for little \nto no chance of precipitation. Occasional general breeziness can \nbe expected across the CWA today. Winds will strengthen tomorrow \nas a potent upper level trough begins to dig inland from the \nPacific Coast, creating increased southwesterly flow aloft. A \ntightening surface pressure gradient over Southwest MT will favor \nthe stronger winds occurring south of I-90, particularly in the \nNorth-South oriented valleys. \n\nOn Saturday, the trough continues to move eastward over the Great\nBasin, breaking down the ridge and putting us in more southerly \nflow aloft. Increased moisture aloft will be caught up in and \ntransported north along this southerly flow, leading to increased \nchances of precipitation over Southwest MT. The ridge breakdown \nwill also allow for temperatures to decrease, creating a cooling \ntrend that we will see throughout the remainder of this forecast \nperiod. This trough is fast moving, however, quickly moving \ntowards the southern US plains by Sunday and allowing for weak \nridging to build back in by Sunday afternoon. This will create a \nday of little to no precipitation chances and partly sunny skies, \nalthough due to the weak nature of the ridge temperatures will \nremain closer to seasonally average. \n\nOn Monday, a very deep upper level trough begins to move inland \nthrough Southern California, creating strong southwesterly flow \naloft. From here is where the uncertainty begins. At this time, \nmodels are showing more agreement in the trough weakening as it \nmoves northeast through the rest of the work week, followed by a \nstrong closed upper level low moving inland off the Pacific Coast.\nHowever, some models are showing the initial trough weakening so \nmuch it begins to completely break down, whereas others show it \nremaining a weak closed low. Some models show the second, stronger\nclosed low moving through Montana, whereas others show it \nremaining south of Montana. How these different tracks will impact\nour weather is outlined in the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios \nsection below. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh confidence surrounds the unseasonable warmth through Friday,\nas well as the transition toward ridge breakdown and cooler \ntemperatures this weekend. Confidence is medium to high (50-60%) \nthat Saturday into Sunday will feature low-impact light \nprecipitation largely across SW Montana and the Rockies. Snow \nlevels will remain well above pass levels, and the scattered \nnature of the precipitation indicates most lower-elevation \nlocations will remain dry. \n\nConfidence has lowered regarding any significant or high-impact \nstorm system early next week. While there is moderate confidence \nin a return toward seasonable temperatures, model spread is \nsubstantial regarding how individual waves track and interact:\n\nScenario 1 (Active, Chaotic Pattern): A split-flow regime keeps \nthe primary moisture tap and strongest dynamics displaced to the \nsouth, while a series of weaker northern-branch shortwaves cross \nthe Northern Rockies. This brings periodic light precipitation and\nminor pass-level snow chances, but overall impacts remain \nminimal.\n\nScenario 2 (More Amplified or Consolidated Wave): A shortwave \ndigs more aggressively or consolidates into a broader trough over \nthe Plains. This would allow a stronger push of Canadian cold air \nand wrap-around moisture into Central Montana, potentially \nintroducing a brief window for more organized precipitation, \nthough confidence in this evolution remains low. -JR",
